 - **The guard.** A PreToolUse hook (`agent-jobs guard`) *parses* every Bash command instead of matching substrings: quotes, wrappers, `sh -c`, `eval`, substitutions and paths are resolved. It blocks every spelling of the outward actions above, git subcommands that run programs, writes into git's own files, dangerous `git config` keys, git identity overrides, the owner's name in a commit, `$?` after an unguarded pipe and wait loops that match themselves. A form it cannot resolve around git or gh is blocked, not guessed (a read-only git call in a command substitution, such as `$(git rev-parse HEAD)`, is resolved and checked like the plain call). A here-doc that `cat` or `tee` only writes out is data, and a `*` inside inline code is code: neither is refused for the paths or globs in its text ([BENCHMARK](BENCHMARK.md#real-usage), «Relaxed on 2026-10-25»). Unparsable input fails closed.
 - **Scrubbed git environment.** The wrapper drops inherited `GIT_*`, `PAGER`, `EDITOR`, `VISUAL`, uses a job-only global git config with the job's identity, no system config, and command-line overrides that switch off repository hooks, fsmonitor, the SSH command, credential helpers, the pager and the editor.
 - **No nested jobs.** A job never starts, cancels or manages jobs. The wrapper sets `AGENT_JOBS_JOB=<id>` on every job; under it, or when an ancestor process or the session leader is a job wrapper (Linux, `/proc`), the binary refuses every command except the job's own MCP server, the guard hook and the wrapper. The guard also rejects `agent-jobs` as a command in any form except `agent-jobs mcp --role job`, and any change to `AGENT_JOBS_*`. After claude exits, the wrapper ends every other process of the job's session (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL) before it reports the job finished, so what the job started in its session does not outlive it.
-- **Cgroup containment (Linux).** With a systemd user manager each job runs in its own transient scope, `agent-jobs-<agent>-<id>.scope`. A cgroup cannot be left with `setsid`, a double fork or re-parenting: control commands refuse inside a job scope, the wrapper ends every other process of the scope before it reports the job finished, and cancel, timeout and collection stop the whole unit. The job loses the user bus variables, and the guard rejects every command that starts processes outside the scope (`systemd-run`, `systemctl --user`, D-Bus tools, `crontab`, `at`, `tmux`, `screen`, `pm2`, `docker`/`podman`, `ssh`, `machinectl`, `loginctl`, `unshare`, `nsenter`; owner decision 2026-10-17) — and, because a job never reaches remote hosts (#116), `scp` and `sftp` outright and `rsync` when an `-e`/`--rsh` command or a `host:path` argument can take it to one (a purely local `rsync`, both paths local, is allowed) — and writes under `/sys/fs/cgroup` in the spellings it resolves (cleaned, relative, symlinked, split by an unset variable, ANSI-C quoted). The guard is not a sandbox: a script the job writes itself is not inspected. `status`, `list`, `job_status` and the job-done line show the containment (`scope <unit>` / `session only`); a failed scope stop is retried every tick. Without `systemd-run --user` (macOS, containers, no user bus) only the session-based checks above apply, which is **weaker**: a process that calls `setsid` and drops the marker escapes them. Config `cgroup_scope`: `auto` (default), `required` (a job without a scope is refused; the Linux installer sets it where scopes work), `off`.
